+## Separate impact and protections
+
+
+The generic memory-corruption consequences remain conditional on reaching the
+vulnerable CIFS client operation. Kernel compromise and cross-VPS access are
+possible consequences of the primitive; the reviewed primary record and patches
+do not establish a complete working exploit on this platform. A failed trigger
+can cause an oops or panic. The operator-confirmed absence of the necessary
+workload prevents this trigger in the assessed scope, rather than mitigating
+an otherwise reached double-free. Generic consequence ratings in `impact` do
+not assert observed platform exposure or a production exploit.
+
+Allocator freelist hardening/randomization, zeroing and stack protection can
+raise exploitation difficulty or detect failed corruption. They do not repair
+response ownership. Monitored kernel warnings are detection and availability
+evidence. The verified mainline/7.1 correction fixes this defect; the three
+reviewed LTS backports do not.
+
+## Runtime, history and mitigation evidence
+
+The global history scope remains 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z. The independently reviewed
+Node126 production-entry override is retained below, without a new override or
+fabricated historical snapshot. `CONFIG_CIFS: enabled`, the sysctl declaration
+and all empty mitigation-acceptance arrays are unchanged. Exclusion follows
+from the operator-confirmed workload absence and mount authority gate, not an
+invented kernel or runtime mitigation identity.
+
+The same frozen evidence is reused. Role dispatch precedes otherwise unavailable
+kernel freshness/history for excluded workloads; this does not supply missing
+kernel facts. No raw evidence, historical-attestation file, source-version rule
+or submission baseline is changed. JSON schema is aligned to the already
+supported empty role array without a dossier schema-version or API change.
+
+## Effective-stable-fix exception
+
+The CNA names1665f25b1dea30bf2d02e16245d203a944c9d994 /6.12.96. Full stable
+patch hunk actually resets response state in send_set_info(), while the
+SMB2_query_directory() replay loop still lacks reset in pinned6.12.113
+1dfea587406b7dd79973831d1677fb2418e5e9aa. It frees resp_buftype/rsp and can
+return to replay_again without clearing them. Thus6.12.fixed_in is explicitly
+null, no effective fix verified through.113; the official claim is disputed
+provenance rather than a safe-version rule. The mainline9647492b5e41954be59d5157eddbcd4cdc1656f7
+patch was independently read from kernel.googlesource.com and correctly targets
+SMB2_query_directory. Exact source, diff, URL and SHA-256 are retained in the
+owned64387-source-correction.json and fixes directory. No empty patch is evidence.
+Architect/lead accepted this bounded source correction. Other stable branch
+fixes require their own hunk checks; no global inference from this one drift.
+
+Other-branch verification completed: the6.6.145 correction (exact full SHA in primary/branch receipt) changes SMB311_posix_qfs_info,
+not QUERY_DIRECTORY. The6.18.39 correction00b0fa425941438b664950a8ee65dfba2def4336
+also changes send_set_info. Those branches therefore have fixed_in:null.
+The7.1.4 patch correctly changes SMB2_query_directory and retains its safe bound.
+The mainline patch correctly changes that function and retains global7.2.
+The review does not assert that no later correction can exist; it records the
+verified bounded source and rejects these three ineffective claimed fixes.
+
+## Superseded version-only evaluation checkpoint
+
+The consumed assessment used the frozen schema 8 snapshot collected at
+2026-10-19T21:48:39Z, byte SHA-256
+`61904e984266df2f6c53435b2b026b0d0980b35a23ed843eebf95b23c27c9274`.
