+# CVE-2026-74586 — SCTP ASCONF new_transport use-after-free
+
+Reviewed 2026-10-19. This dossier assesses the tenant-configured networking path; it is not a claim about every host service or every historical node configuration. The per-node evaluation is a separate evidence product.
+
+## Primary defect and evidence strength
+
+An authenticated ASCONF can add a transport and delete it again through wildcard DEL-IP while leaving asoc->new_transport dangling. The cached pointer is then used for a queued HEARTBEAT; after RCU frees the transport, successful local-address replacement releases the heartbeat and sctp_outq_select_transport reads the freed transport state. The fix clears new_transport when that peer is removed.
+
+The upstream report describes a KASAN reproducer succeeding in 2/2 unpatched runs, with a four-byte read of transport->state. That is a concrete stale-object read, not a demonstrated arbitrary write or complete privilege-escalation chain. CNA Critical scoring is kept separate from that narrower evidence.
+
+Linux CNA cvssV3_1: 9.8 (CRITICAL), `CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H`. The score and any CNA scenario assertions are source attribution, not a demonstrated vpsAdminOS escape or a substitute for the prerequisite analysis.
+
+## Introduction, stable fixes and exceptions
+
+The primary Linux CNA record places the global introduction at **3.2.0**, commit [`6af29ccc223b0feb6fc6112281c3fa3cdb1afddf`](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=6af29ccc223b0feb6fc6112281c3fa3cdb1afddf), and the mainline fix at **7.2.0**, commit [`beb33f8ee1ca83acddb2a5ae80f3d22ec550b4c3`](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?id=beb33f8ee1ca83acddb2a5ae80f3d22ec550b4c3). The 6.12 correction is [`291accf36febce751021888de5f15090f4875b56`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/291accf36febce751021888de5f15090f4875b56) in **6.12.104**.
+
