+# CVE-2026-74630 — IPv6 inet6_dev reference resurrection during device teardown
+
+Reviewed 2026-10-19. This dossier assesses the tenant-configured networking path; it is not a claim about every host service or every historical node configuration. The per-node evaluation is a separate evidence product.
+
+## Primary defect and evidence strength
+
+in6_dev_get loaded dev->ip6_ptr under RCU then unconditionally incremented its reference count. Device teardown could clear the pointer and drop the last reference between those operations. The getter resurrected an object already queued for RCU free; the caller later used freed state. The fix uses refcount_inc_not_zero and returns NULL when the last reference has gone.
+
+The upstream report attributes an independent UID1000 reproduction on exact unpatched7.2-rc3 revision6f5156d7a31a, followed by a refcount underflow. Its supplied trace records an eight-byte KASAN write in mutex_lock after the RCU read section. This demonstrates the bug on that tested upstream build, not a vpsAdminOS host exploit.
+
+Linux CNA cvssV3_1: 7.8 (HIGH), `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H`. The score and any CNA scenario assertions are source attribution, not a demonstrated vpsAdminOS escape or a substitute for the prerequisite analysis.
+
+## Introduction, stable fixes and exceptions
+
