+- [6.12 stable correction](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/fca7e444c04689fe4cc6b56f2725f804a4bb1ef9), first fixed 6.12.105
+
+Formal metrics are Linux CNA CVSS source ratings, not a custom container score.
+Their exploit-consequence discussion is attributed primary analysis, not proof
+that the vpsAdminOS hardening/build was exploited:
+
+```json
+[
+  {
+    "cvssV3_1": {
+      "version": "3.1",
+      "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:H/A:H",
+      "baseScore": 9.4,
+      "baseSeverity": "CRITICAL"
+    },
+    "scenarios": [
+      {
+        "lang": "en",
+        "value": "AV:N - The bug is reached by sending crafted TCP/MPTCP segments to a listening socket over the network (ip4/ip6 receive -> tcp_conn_request/tcp_rcv_state_process -> subflow_prep_synack or mptcp_incoming_options -> check_fully_established); per kernel CNA guidance, net stack packet-handling paths are Network.\nAC:L - In the intended passive-MPTFO deployment (MPTCP listener with net.ipv4.tcp_fastopen server bit set), the attacker controls obtaining a valid TFO cookie and then sending a data-less SYN followed by an out-of-order MPC_ACK to deterministically hit the stale is_mptfo path in check_fully_established().\nPR:N - Exploitation requires only unauthenticated inbound TCP connections to an MPTCP listener; the attacker obtains the TFO cookie from their own prior connection and needs no local account, capability, or namespace privileges on the target host.\nUI:N - No victim interaction is required beyond the server normally accepting TCP connections; the attacker programmatically sends the SYN and follow-up MPC_ACK packets without anyone opening files, clicking links, or performing other actions.\nS:U - Impact is confined to kernel MPTCP/TCP connection state on the target host and does not cross VM, container, IOMMU, or other security boundaries; it is not a sandbox or guest-to-host escape.\nC:L - Premature fully_established with attacker-chosen MPTCP keys desynchronizes handshake and DSS/map state, enabling misdelivery or premature exposure of connection-level data/metadata to the remote peer before normal MP_CAPABLE validation completes.\nI:H - Stale is_mptfo lets an out-of-order MPC_ACK force __mptcp_subflow_fully_established(), writing attacker-controlled sndr_key/ack_seq into msk and subflow crypto state and corrupting subsequent DSS/ACK handling on the server multipath socket.\nA:H - Data-less passive-TFO SYNs hit WARN_ON_ONCE(!skb) on every trigger, and the resulting inconsistent is_mptfo/fully_established state can cause repeatable kernel WARN/oops (panic_on_warn panic) plus connection reset storms against internet-facing MPTCP servers."
+      }
