+# CVE-2026-89930: KVM failed nested VM-entry stale TLB
+
+## Decision and source scope
+
+Approved member-relevance assessment; unpublished draft candidate. [Primary Linux CNA record](https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/linux/security/vulns/+/56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9/cve/published/2026/CVE-2026-89930.json) is PUBLISHED at snapshot `56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9`, reviewed on 2026-10-19. This assessment does not claim production exploitation or a completed platform exploit test. The authenticated preflight found no existing CVE/external-ID or local dossier duplicate in its 56-advisory/57-CVE scope.
+
+## Formal severity
+
+The primary Linux CNA container supplies CVSS 3.1, score 9.3 (CRITICAL), vector `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H`. These formal source scores are separate from the platform outcomes below. The snapshot has no per-record update timestamp; its exact source revision and review date bind this assessment.
+
+## Defect and correction
+
+A failed nested VM-entry updated last_vpid or queued local TLB flushes after switching to L2 context without servicing those flushes. A following successful entry could then reuse stale mappings. The fix services kvm_service_local_tlb_flush_requests in vmentry_fail_vmexit_guest_mode before leave_guest_mode. This concerns nested address-translation semantics, not an arbitrary host write demonstrated in the primary record.
+
+Mainline introduction: `4.4.0`, commit [`5c614b3583e7b6dab0c86356fa36c2bcbb8322a0`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/5c614b3583e7b6dab0c86356fa36c2bcbb8322a0). Mainline fixing commit: [`05a0b701d1089fb57beeb8982f23c3bbafe0fa8b`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/05a0b701d1089fb57beeb8982f23c3bbafe0fa8b), first fixed final mainline release `7.3.0`. Stable first correction in the requested inclusive range: `6.12.110`. The CNA Git intervals, semantic versions and CPE applicability were checked together; this is not a CVE-number/date cutoff.
