+# CVE-2026-89932: KVM nested VPID reuse across VM lifetimes
+
+## Decision and source scope
+
+Approved member-relevance assessment; unpublished draft candidate. [Primary Linux CNA record](https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/linux/security/vulns/+/56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9/cve/published/2026/CVE-2026-89932.json) is PUBLISHED at snapshot `56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9`, reviewed on 2026-10-19. This assessment does not claim production exploitation or a completed platform exploit test. The authenticated preflight found no existing CVE/external-ID or local dossier duplicate in its 56-advisory/57-CVE scope.
+
+## Formal severity
+
+The primary Linux CNA container supplies CVSS 3.1, score 8.8 (HIGH), vector `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H`. These formal source scores are separate from the platform outcomes below. The snapshot has no per-record update timestamp; its exact source revision and review date bind this assessment.
+
+## Defect and correction
+
+last_vpid was initialized only at vCPU creation, not when VMXOFF released vpid02 and VMXON later allocated it again. Reusing a VPID with translations from an earlier vCPU or completely different VM could let the new L2 use those stale translations. The fix resets last_vpid=0 at allocation so the first nested entry forces invalidation. The explicit prior-VM lifetime supplies a concrete cross-VM mapping mechanism; attacker control of placement/reuse and reliable access remain qualifications.
+
+Mainline introduction: `4.4.0`, commit [`5c614b3583e7b6dab0c86356fa36c2bcbb8322a0`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/5c614b3583e7b6dab0c86356fa36c2bcbb8322a0). Mainline fixing commit: [`f0772389413dce9657c7d6950abf3edbbd511356`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f0772389413dce9657c7d6950abf3edbbd511356), first fixed final mainline release `7.3.0`. Stable first correction in the requested inclusive range: `6.12.110`. The CNA Git intervals, semantic versions and CPE applicability were checked together; this is not a CVE-number/date cutoff.
