+# CVE-2026-99999: IPv4 checksum metadata header corruption
+
+## Decision and source scope
+
+Approved member-relevance assessment; unpublished draft candidate. [Primary Linux CNA record](https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/linux/security/vulns/+/56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9/cve/published/2026/CVE-2026-99999.json) is PUBLISHED at snapshot `56fdb3bed3f95b64b83c9f1a42ccb603452ec5c9`, reviewed on 2026-10-19. This assessment does not claim production exploitation or a completed platform exploit test. The authenticated preflight found no existing CVE/external-ID or local dossier duplicate in its 56-advisory/57-CVE scope.
+
+## Formal severity
+
+The primary Linux CNA container supplies CVSS 3.1, score 9.3 (CRITICAL), vector `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H`. These formal source scores are separate from the platform outcomes below. The snapshot has no per-record update timestamp; its exact source revision and review date bind this assessment.
+
+## Defect and correction
+
+Untrusted partial-checksum metadata can place checksum_start before the end of a validated IPv4 header. Deferred checksum completion can then rewrite that header after earlier parsing, leading to unsafe downstream interpretation and kernel memory exposure/corruption. The stable32a8b0ad correction introduces ip_check_csum_start and checks before checksum completion in IPv4 fragmentation, bridge/queue/checksum/XFRM paths. Primary text calls fuller input validation forthcoming: this dossier credits the actual corrective commit, not an invented complete metadata-validation redesign.
+
+Mainline introduction: `2.6.27`, commit [`f43798c27684ab925adde7d8acc34c78c6e50df8`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f43798c27684ab925adde7d8acc34c78c6e50df8). Mainline fixing commit: [`6785011f8b16abf40b1e9d8b2e332232b3e68822`](https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/6785011f8b16abf40b1e9d8b2e332232b3e68822), first fixed final mainline release `7.3.0`. Stable first correction in the requested inclusive range: `6.12.113`. The CNA Git intervals, semantic versions and CPE applicability were checked together; this is not a CVE-number/date cutoff.
