+# Selecting kernel CVEs for assessment
+
+Use this procedure before gathering, selecting, or reconsidering a batch of
+kernel CVEs. It produces a reviewable research selection. Dossier preparation
+and vpsAdmin writes follow only within the user's approved scope, through the
+[assessment workflow](../AGENTS.md), [evidence contract](evidence.md) and
+[API permission rules](api-permissions.md).
+
+## Establish the question and selection policy
+
+Record the requested kernel branches and inclusive release interval, the
+reporting checkpoint, threat breadth, intended readers and authorized phase.
+Read representative existing advisories to understand which threats members
+have previously been told about. Treat that history as evidence of editorial
+scope, not a substitute for the current user's direction.
+
+Choose CVEs by member relevance independently of platform exposure. Serious
+privilege gain, isolation bypass, broad kernel or host compromise and substantial
+shared-host data exposure can merit an assessment. A useful explanation that
+the service was not affected can also merit inclusion: a disabled subsystem,
+blocked trigger or unavailable device does not automatically make a prominent
+kernel threat irrelevant to members. Explain the connection to tenant-facing
+interfaces, common workloads or a threat members are likely to encounter.
+Unrelated hardware or architecture bugs need a concrete relevance reason;
+a high score alone is insufficient.
+
+Keep formal CVSS separate from this decision. Preserve its authority, version,
+vector, score and source date when supplied; record when it is absent or
+disputed. Do not turn a tentative request for “critical” threats into an
+unexplained score threshold, or treat every memory-safety report as a proven
+host escape. Identify which classification choices need user direction.
+
+Version the selection policy and proposal in the research record with a date
+and source revision. If the policy changes, retain the previous selection,
+exposure claims and reasons, then explain the revision. A broader relevance
+policy does not establish that the platform was historically vulnerable.
+
+## Freeze sources and enumerate the complete interval
+
+Pin the primary Linux CNA snapshot, stable release commits and downstream
+Linux/vpsAdminOS revisions. Record source URLs, retrieval times and hashes or
+immutable revisions. A branch name or a version string alone does not identify
+a custom build. Pin vpsAdmin, configuration and container-runtime sources when
+they supply prerequisites used in the analysis.
+
+For fixes first shipped from release A through release B inclusive, enumerate
+the stable graph from A's immediate predecessor, excluded, through B, included.
+Verify exact endpoint identities and ancestry. A range starting at A itself
+would miss A's fixes. Preserve per-release counts, including zero-hit releases.
+
+Join primary CVE records to exact stable corrective commits and first containing
+releases. Do not use CVE numbers, publication dates, the newest advisory's CVE
+or current deployed kernel as range cutoffs. Check commit references as well as
+version intervals: semantic-version omissions and backports can make either
+source incomplete. Record mainline and stable-branch introduction/fix boundaries;
+older stable backports can introduce a bug before its mainline introduction.
+
+Handle these cases explicitly:
+
+- Later CVE assignments to previously unassigned security fixes: match upstream
+  and downstream commits, not just titles. Keep still-unassigned fixes in a
+  separate appendix, with the snapshot and search scope supporting that result.
+- Ineffective fixes and later corrections: distinguish the CNA's original fix,
+  the actual stable correction, downstream backport and first mitigating live
+  patch. Explain range exceptions rather than silently rewriting boundaries.
+- Rejected or withdrawn records: retain their status, source and disposition,
+  including any replacement ID. Do not count them as active proposed CVEs.
+- Existing livepatch coverage: begin with the boot kernel's canonical
+  `docs/os/livepatches/` page in vpsAdminOS, then verify current primary records
+  and source. Historical inclusion, qualification for deployment and actual
+  activation are separate facts. A page's old assignment check is not a current
+  exhaustive CVE inventory.
+
+Separate mechanical enumeration from focused manual impact review. Give every
+enumerated record a disposition or an explicit unresolved status. Report how
+many received individual source review, which rules screened the remainder,
+and what remains unreviewed. A complete mechanical join does not mean every
+candidate's exploitability was manually established.
+
+## Establish the complete prior-report set
+
+Find the latest published advisory and its publication timestamp, then enumerate
+all public advisories and CVE associations. Verify filtering, pagination and
+count metadata rather than assuming a limit parameter was honored. Deduplicate
+against the complete association set, repository dossiers and submission
+baselines, plus private drafts accessible within the authorized read scope.
+
+Public reads cannot rule out private drafts. Record unavailable reads and
+resolve the remaining duplicate check before an approved draft is created.
+Keep “CNA record is published” separate from “already reported in vpsAdmin.”
+Inspect all relevant remote advisory states, including retracted advisories;
+an existing record needs an explicit update/replacement decision, not an
+automatic second draft. Likewise, two defects in the same subsystem are not
+duplicates merely because their summaries resemble each other.
+
+## Assess relevance and exposure separately
+
+For each candidate, derive the vulnerable operation and its attacker
+prerequisites from the primary record and fix. Trace those prerequisites through
+the actual downstream source. Check the precise interface and privilege scope:
+
+- initial-user-namespace authority versus delegated container-userns capabilities;
+- built-in functionality, module compilation, explicit preload, automatic loading
+  and actual loaded modules;
+- device delegation and per-VPS features, syscall filters and LSM policy;
+- protocol negotiation, peer control, mounts and unusual network configuration;
+- architecture, hardware features, node role and real host-service workloads.
+
+A container administrator's ability to configure an optional feature can make
+its trigger relevant even when ordinary traffic does not use it. Conversely,
+compiling or preloading a filesystem does not grant permission to mount it in
+a user namespace. Storage and other host-service roles require their own
+workload analysis; tenant-only triggers cannot be assigned to them merely
+because they run an older kernel.
+
+Separate root inside a VPS, host-root or kernel execution, cross-VPS access and
+host availability. For each claimed consequence state whether cited research
+demonstrates it, a specific primitive makes it plausible, or the path remains
+unresolved. Identify the reviewed source or test scope behind a negative
+statement. Neither absent logs nor this investigation can establish that
+production exploitation did not occur.
+
+Explain whether a protection prevents the trigger, makes exploitation harder,
+detects failed attempts or fixes the bug. Allocator hardening is not a fix;
+monitored warnings are detection and availability evidence.
+
+## Bound positive and negative platform conclusions
+
+Keep current exposure, historical affected intervals and mitigation state
+separate. A fixed current kernel does not establish that earlier kernels were
+never affected. Record exact builds, configurations, dates, versions, profiles,
+node roles and coverage limits for every conclusion.
+
+Use `not_affected` in research only with positive evidence that a necessary
+prerequisite was absent throughout that stated scope. For example, an exact
+archived build with a required subsystem disabled can support a conclusion
+about that build. A verified profile that necessarily blocks the only trigger
+can support a conclusion about that profile. Neither proves that every deployed
+node or historical interval used that build or profile.
+
+An uninspected override, missing module sample, undocumented device policy,
+source default, absent log or lack of a published exploit cannot prove fleet
+non-exposure. State the evidence gap and required follow-up. Match deployed
+claims to booted identity, effective configuration and relevant runtime facts,
+not merely the activated system's source or a repository default. Keep partial
+coverage explicit instead of extending a conclusion to unexamined nodes.
+
+The [evidence contract](evidence.md) remains authoritative for dossier evaluation.
+Research uncertainty does not change its conservative rules: after the platform
+reachability review, missing historical configuration on an affected release
+is treated as affected unless exact evidence proves the trigger unavailable.
+Missing baselines or other unresolved evidence can still block evaluation.
+Fixed and pre-introduction stable releases can be classified without
+configuration evidence; custom-build and runtime-mitigation overrides require
+their exact identities and supporting evidence. Do not manufacture historical
+attestations or carry an active livepatch across a reboot without the required
+transition evidence.
+
+## Keep a reviewable selection record
+
+Use the existing research layout and a readable table or structured record; this
+procedure adds no required file format, API field, schema or evaluator state.
+For each considered candidate preserve:
+
+| Item | Required reasoning or provenance |
+| --- | --- |
+| Identity and range | CVE/status, primary snapshot and URL, introduction boundaries, stable fix SHA and first release, correction exceptions and known downstream correction |
+| Threat | Primitive, attacker prerequisites, separate claimed outcomes and evidence strength, formal CVSS with its source when available |
+| Selection | `propose`, `conditional_review`, `defer` or `exclude`, with a member-relevance reason independent of exposure |
+| Exposure | `affected`, `not_affected`, `conditional`, `unknown` or `unassessed`, with evidence and exact build/profile/role/time scope |
+| History and mitigation | Current facts, historical interval, first actual fix/mitigation, partial coverage and unknowns kept distinct |
+| Prior reports | Public associations, local dossiers, accessible private drafts, previous claims and explicit read limits |
+| Revision and approval | Policy/proposal revision, previous decision and reason for change, approval wording/scope and next evidence needed |
+
+These selection/exposure labels describe research decisions. They are not new
+values for the evaluator or vpsAdmin, and cannot replace a dossier's supported
+per-node states. Keep raw private-node evidence and credentials out of public
+research, committed prose and URLs; follow the existing ignored evidence
+storage contract when approved assessment work needs it.
+
+Before presenting the proposal, check endpoint identities, fix membership,
+status changes, duplicate IDs and prior-report joins. Resolve citation links and
+check that every conclusion has the claimed scope. Present the exact proposed
+IDs with reasons, useful negative assessments, conditional cases, borderline
+impact and deferred questions. State what completeness claim the evidence
+supports and what remains unresolved.
+
+## Observe the phase and approval boundaries
+
+| Phase | Authorization and completion boundary |
+| --- | --- |
+| Research and selection | Gather the authorized evidence and present the proposal. A research/list request does not authorize advisory dossiers or remote drafts. |
+| Dossier and draft preparation | Obtain user selection approval before entering this phase when the request was research-only. Record the exact approved IDs, proposal/policy revision and scope; follow the existing assessment, evaluation, review and guarded sync workflow. |
+| Publication | Require separate explicit approval for each exact reviewed advisory content revision. Use the existing read-only publication preflight and guarded publication command; a successful readiness check is not publication approval. |
+| Email | Require separate explicit email authorization. The publication command keeps `send_mail: false`; do not infer notification permission from publication approval. |
+
